==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰuH-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|probable}}, from {{der|en|fro|probable}}, from {{der|en|la|probābilis|t=that may be proved, credible}}, from {{m|la|probāre|t=to test, examine}}; see {{m|en|probe}}, {{m|en|probity}}, {{m|en|prove}}. Compare recent doublet {{m|en|provable}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹɒbəbl̩/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹɑbəbl̩/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-probable.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|prob|a|ble}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[likely|Likely]] or most likely to be [[true]].
#: {{ux|en|It's '''probable''' that it will rain tomorrow.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''probable''' source of the failure was the mass of feathers in the intake manifold.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1959 October|author=Michael Robbins|title=The Isle of Wight (Newport Junction) Railway|journal=Railway Magazine|page=721|text=A major accident and embezzlement of its funds seem to have been the only disasters it escaped; and as to the latter, the '''most probable''' reason is that it never had any quantity of funds worth embezzling.}}
# Likely to [[happen]].
#: {{ux|en|With all the support we have, success is looking '''probable'''.}}
# Supporting, or giving ground for, belief, but not demonstrating.
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|passage='''probable''' evidence}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Golden Bough|author=James George Frazer|year=1890|volume=2|page=8|text=From an examination of the Teutonic words for "temple" Grimm has made it '''probable''' that amongst the Germans the oldest sanctuaries were natural woods.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Capable of being [[prove]]d.

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|improbable}}

